# THE BUG LEDGER NO LONGER NEEDS A MERGE DRIVER, and that is the point.
#
# It used to be BUG-HISTORY.md: one file that every code PR was required to
# prepend to, so every open branch edited the same first line. This file carried
# `BUG-HISTORY.md merge=union` (PR #2133) to resolve that silently, and it did —
# but ONLY when our git performs the merge. The attribute is applied by whichever
# git does the merging, and GitHub's git does not read this repository's
# .gitattributes.
#
# That distinction stopped being academic when `main` got a merge QUEUE, which
# stacks entry 2 on entry 1's result using GitHub's git. Measured on the live
# queue 2026-08-20: seven entries, six UNMERGEABLE, all seven touching
# BUG-HISTORY.md. The queue was serialised to one PR at a time by the repo's own
# mandatory rule.
#
# Since 2026-08-20 the ledger is docs/bugs/, ONE FILE PER ENTRY. Two PRs adding
# entries write two different paths, so there is nothing for any git — ours or
# GitHub's — to call a conflict, with or without a merge driver. Do not add one
# back: a driver that only half the merges honour is how this was missed for a
# week. See docs/bugs/README.md.

# docs/generated/* is REGENERATED from source, never hand-edited — so when two
# branches both regenerate it, neither side is right and both are recoverable.
# `union` would be wrong here (it duplicates rows rather than merging them) and
# picking a side leaves a stale file until someone notices.
#
# Measured 2026-08-18 on the 97 live branches: 21 conflict on bug-index.md and
# 13 conflict on NOTHING BUT generated markdown. Nineteen commits in that
# backlog exist only to regenerate after a merge — pure tax.
#
# `regen` takes either side without stopping the merge; scripts/regen-generated.sh
# then rebuilds them from source, which is the only version that is actually
# correct. Enable it once per clone (CI does it in the checkout step):
#
#     git config merge.regen.driver "scripts/regen-generated.sh %A"
#
# Without that config git falls back to a normal merge, i.e. today's behaviour —
# so a clone that has not run it is no worse off, only no better.
docs/generated/*.md  merge=regen
docs/generated/*.csv merge=regen

# The demo payment-advice PDF is all-ASCII (uncompressed streams), so git's
# text auto-detection claims it and autocrlf rewrites its line endings on
# checkout — which shifts every /Length and xref offset and cuts the last
# batch row off the stream. That exact corruption happened in the working
# tree on 2026-08-24: the reader refused the file ("2 batch rows come to
# RM 2,548.87" against a printed RM 11,814.44). A PDF is bytes, whatever it
# happens to contain; tests/pbbAdviceFixture.test.mjs reads the shipped file
# and fails loudly if it ever rots again.
demo-statements/*.pdf -text
